'''Richard Michael DeWine'''<ref>{{Cite web|title=Former Sen. Mike DeWine - R Ohio|url=https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/51174/Richard_Michael_DeWine.html|publisher=LegiStorm}}</ref> ({{IPAc-en|d|ə|'|w|aɪ|n}} {{respell|də|WYNE}};<ref>{{cite AV media |date= April 14, 2022|title= Mike DeWine – Took Action|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XwvtN8M4nmY|publisher=DeWine Husted for Ohio|via=[[YouTube]]|access-date=August 20, 2024 |time= 00:04}}</ref> born January 5, 1947) is an American politician and attorney serving as the 70th [[List of governors of Ohio|governor of Ohio]] since 2019. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he served as the 50th [[Attorney General of Ohio|attorney general of Ohio]] from 2011 to 2019, as a [[United States Senate|United States senator from Ohio]] from 1995 to 2007, and in the [[U.S. House of Representatives]] from 1983 to 1991.

A native of [[Yellow Springs, Ohio]], DeWine graduated from [[Miami University]] in 1969 and [[Pettit College of Law|Ohio Northern University College of Law]] in 1972. He was an assistant prosecutor in [[Greene County, Ohio]], and served one term as county prosecutor from 1977 to 1981. He continued his political career in the [[Ohio Senate]] from 1981 to 1982, and then served four terms as a U.S. representative from 1983 until 1991. From 1991 to 1994, he served as the 59th [[List of lieutenant governors of Ohio|lieutenant governor of Ohio]], under Governor [[George Voinovich]].

DeWine was [[1994 United States Senate election in Ohio|elected to the U.S. Senate]] in a landslide during the 1994 [[Republican Revolution]], and sat on the Senate [[United States Senate Committee on Appropriations|Appropriations]]; [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Judiciary]]; [[United States Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor and Pensions|Health, Education, Labor and Pensions (HELP)]]; and [[United States Senate Select Committee on Intelligence|Select Intelligence]] Committees. He was reelected [[2000 United States Senate election in Ohio|in 2000]] and defeated [[2006 United States Senate election in Ohio|in 2006]] by Democrat [[Sherrod Brown]]. DeWine returned to politics four years later as the 50th attorney general of Ohio. In that office, he challenged the [[Affordable Care Act]] and tackled the [[opioid epidemic]]. He was elected governor [[2018 Ohio gubernatorial election|in 2018]] and reelected [[2022 Ohio gubernatorial election|in 2022]].<ref>{{Cite web|author=Aditi Sangal, Adrienne Vogt, Elise Hammond, Maureen Chowdhury, Clare Foran, Meg Wagner, Melissa Macaya, Joe Ruiz and Seán Federico-OMurchú|date=November 8, 2022|title=CNN Projection: Republican Gov. Mike DeWine will win reelection in Ohio|url=https://www.cnn.com/politics/live-news/midterm-election-results-livestream-voting-11-08-2022/h_6dab3e1f3532b3062bc489d6ab42108d|access-date=November 9, 2022|website=CNN|language=en|archive-date=January 8, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230108214214/https://www.cnn.com/politics/live-news/midterm-election-results-livestream-voting-11-08-2022/h_6dab3e1f3532b3062bc489d6ab42108d|url-status=live}}</ref>

During DeWine's tenure as governor, the [[2019 Dayton shooting]] prompted him to urge the Ohio legislature to enact new gun control measures, such as expanding background checks and harsher penalties for those in possession of unregistered firearms. In early 2020, DeWine received national attention for his response to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Ohio]], ordering the closing of dine-in restaurant service and sporting events and delegating additional resources to elderly care facilities.<ref name="Witte">{{cite news|title=Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine's coronavirus response has become a national guide to the crisis|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/national/coronavirus-ohio-dewine-outbreak/2020/03/16/9bde6b1e-67b2-11ea-9923-57073adce27c_story.html|last1=Witte|first1=Griff|last2=Zezima|first2=Katie|date=March 16, 2020|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200317142655/https://www.washingtonpost.com/national/coronavirus-ohio-dewine-outbreak/2020/03/16/9bde6b1e-67b2-11ea-9923-57073adce27c_story.html|archive-date=March 17, 2020|access-date=March 17, 2020}}</ref>

==Early life, education, and career==
[[File:Mike DeWine and family pose with Ronald Reagan.jpg|thumb|left|Congressman DeWine (far-right) and his family with President [[Ronald Reagan]] in 1985]]

DeWine was born in [[Springfield, Ohio]], on January 5, 1947, and grew up in nearby [[Yellow Springs, Ohio|Yellow Springs]].<ref>{{CongBio|id = D000294|name = DeWine, Michael|inline = yes}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url = https://www.nytimes.com/2024/09/20/opinion/springfield-haitian-migrants-ohio.html|title = I'm the Republican Governor of Ohio. Here Is the Truth About Springfield|last = DeWine|first = Mike|date = September 20, 2024|accessdate = September 20, 2024|newspaper = [[The New York Times]]|url-access = limited|quote = I was born in Springfield, Ohio.}}</ref> He is the son of Jean Ruth ({{nee}} Liddle) and Richard Lee DeWine.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://freepages.genealogy.rootpsweb.com/~battle/senators/dewine.htm|title=1|work=rootsweb.com|access-date=February 11, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080307045513/http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~battle/senators/dewine.htm|archive-date=March 7, 2008|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.legacy.com/obituaries/dayton/obituary.aspx?n=jean-ruth-and-richard-lee-dewine&pid=119626791|title=Jean DeWine Obituary – Dayton, OH|website=legacy.com|access-date=February 11, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190421155346/https://www.legacy.com/obituaries/dayton/obituary.aspx?n=jean-ruth-and-richard-lee-dewine&pid=119626791|archive-date=April 21, 2019|url-status=live|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://ysnews.com/news/2018/11/hometown-candidate-dewine-aims-for-top-state-office|title=Hometown candidate – DeWine aims for top state office|first=Megan|last=Bachman|date=November 8, 2018|website=The Yellow Springs News|access-date=February 11, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116081803/https://ysnews.com/news/2018/11/hometown-candidate-dewine-aims-for-top-state-office|archive-date=November 16, 2018|url-status=live|language=en}}</ref> He is a [[Catholic]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.franciscanmedia.org/us-senator-mike-dewine-pro-life-pro-children-pro-family/|title=US Senator Mike DeWine: Pro-life, Pro-children, Pro-family|date=August 31, 2016|work=Franciscan Media|access-date=October 3, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181004021323/https://www.franciscanmedia.org/us-senator-mike-dewine-pro-life-pro-children-pro-family/|archive-date=October 4, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ohiocathconf.org/Contact/Legislative-Contacts|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181003204605/https://www.ohiocathconf.org/Contact/Legislative-Contacts|url-status=dead|archive-date=October 3, 2018|title=Contact your Legislators – Catholic Conference of Ohio|date=October 3, 2018|access-date=October 3, 2018}}</ref><ref name="crainscleveland">{{Cite web|url=https://www.crainscleveland.com/article/20140728/CUSTOM03/307289999/mike-dewine-takes-a-rare-political-misstep|title=Mike DeWine takes a rare political misstep|website=Crain's Cleveland Business|date=April 8, 2014|access-date=October 3, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181004021526/https://www.crainscleveland.com/article/20140728/CUSTOM03/307289999/mike-dewine-takes-a-rare-political-misstep|archive-date=October 4, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> DeWine earned a [[Bachelor of Science]] degree in education from [[Miami University]] in 1969 and a [[Juris Doctor]] from [[Pettit College of Law|Ohio Northern University College of Law]] in 1972.

At age 25, DeWine started working as an assistant prosecuting attorney for [[Greene County, Ohio]], and in 1976 he was elected county prosecutor, serving four years.<ref>{{cite news|last=Gomez|first=Henry J.|url=http://www.cleveland.com/open/index.ssf/2014/10/ohio_attorney_general_mike_dew_4.html|title=Ohio Attorney General Mike DeWine looks back on first term and ahead to possible run for governor: Q&A|newspaper=[[The Plain Dealer]]|date=October 14, 2014|access-date=September 13, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151004061734/http://www.cleveland.com/open/index.ssf/2014/10/ohio_attorney_general_mike_dew_4.html|archive-date=October 4, 2015|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=senate/><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.newspapers.com/image/42863444/|title=U.S. Senate Unchanged|date=November 3, 1976|page=1|newspaper=Xenia Daily Gazette|via=Newspapers.com|access-date=February 11, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190212012837/http://www.newspapers.com/image/42863444|archive-date=February 12, 2019|url-status=live|url-access=limited}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.newspapers.com/image/395599894/|title=New faces appear on totem pole of public life|date=January 1, 1981|newspaper=The Journal Herald|via=Newspapers.com|access-date=February 11, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181229075824/https://www.newspapers.com/image/395599894/|archive-date=December 29, 2018|url-status=live|url-access=limited}}</ref>

In 1980, DeWine was elected to the [[Ohio State Senate]] and served one two-year term.<ref name=senate>{{cite news|last=Johnson|first=Alan|url=http://www.dispatch.com/content/stories/local/2014/09/20/dewine-pepper-are-vastly-different.html|title=Attorney general candidates DeWine, Pepper are vastly different|newspaper=[[The Columbus Dispatch]]|date=September 21, 2014|access-date=September 13, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150923234610/http://www.dispatch.com/content/stories/local/2014/09/20/dewine-pepper-are-vastly-different.html|archive-date=September 23, 2015|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Bush Contact Sheet P17133 (cropped).jpg|thumb|DeWine as U.S. representative with President [[George H. W. Bush]] in 1990]]

In 1982, U.S. representative [[Bud Brown (politician)|Bud Brown]] of [[Ohio's 7th congressional district]] retired after 18 years in Congress; his father, [[Clarence Brown, Sr.]], had held the seat for 26 years before that. DeWine won the Republican nomination, assuring his election in November. He was reelected three more times from this district, which stretched from his home in Springfield to the [[Columbus, Ohio|Columbus]] suburbs. He ran unopposed in 1986 in what was regarded as a bad year for Republicans nationally.

In 1986, DeWine was one of the [[House impeachment managers]] who prosecuted the case in the [[Federal impeachment trial in the United States|impeachment trial]] of Judge [[Harry E. Claiborne]]. Claiborne was found guilty by the [[United States Senate]] and removed from his federal judgeship.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://history.house.gov/Institution/Impeachment/Impeachment-List/|title=List of Individuals Impeached by the House of Representatives|publisher=[[United States House of Representatives]]|access-date=January 15, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191218232339/https://history.house.gov/Institution/Impeachment/Impeachment-List/|archive-date=December 18, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref>

DeWine did not seek reelection to the House of Representatives in 1990, and briefly ran for governor, but withdrew before the primaries and instead ran<ref>{{cite web | url=https://audioboom.com/posts/8066426-that-so-cincinnati-former-ohio-gov-bob-taft-shares-his-thoughts-on-today-s-politics-donald-tru | title=That So Cincinnati: Former Ohio Gov. Bob Taft shares his thoughts on today's politics, Donald Trump and death penalty | access-date=July 6, 2024 | archive-date=July 6, 2024 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240706205152/https://audioboom.com/posts/8066426-that-so-cincinnati-former-ohio-gov-bob-taft-shares-his-thoughts-on-today-s-politics-donald-tru | url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="DeWineTributes2007">{{cite web |url=https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CDOC-109sdoc30/pdf/CDOC-109sdoc30.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240305031452/https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CDOC-109sdoc30/pdf/CDOC-109sdoc30.pdf |archive-date=March 5, 2024 |title=Tributes to Hon. Mike DeWine |publisher=U.S. Government Printing Office |date=2007 |location=Washington, D.C. |access-date=February 10, 2025}}</ref> for [[lieutenant governor of Ohio|lieutenant governor]] as [[George Voinovich]]'s [[running mate]] in [[1990 Ohio gubernatorial election|that year's Ohio gubernatorial election]]. The Voinovich–DeWine ticket was easily elected.

==U.S. Senate (1995–2007)==
[[File:President George W. Bush signs H.R. 5403.jpg|thumb|Senator DeWine (second from right) attending the signing of the Safe and Timely Interstate Placement of Foster Children Act by President [[George W. Bush]] in 2006]]

[[1992 United States Senate election in Ohio|In 1992]], DeWine unsuccessfully ran for United States Senate against former astronaut and three-term incumbent [[John Glenn]]. His campaign used the phrase "What ''on earth'' has John Glenn done?", echoing [[Jeff Bingaman]]'s slogan "What on Earth has he done for you lately?" against former astronaut [[Harrison Schmitt]] in the [[1982 United States Senate election in New Mexico]].<ref>Clifford Krauss{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/10/15/us/in-big-re-election-fight-glenn-tests-hero-image.html|title=In Big Re-election Fight, Glenn Tests Hero Image|newspaper=The New York Times|date=October 15, 1992|access-date=July 21, 2008|first=Clifford|last=Krauss|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090605140253/http://www.nytimes.com/1992/10/15/us/in-big-re-election-fight-glenn-tests-hero-image.html|archive-date=June 5, 2009|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine|url=http://www.time.com/time/specials/packages/article/0,28804,1910599_1910769_1910767-3,00.html|title=40th Anniversary of Apollo 11: Moonstruck|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|date=July 27, 2009|access-date=January 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130718084817/http://www.time.com/time/specials/packages/article/0,28804,1910599_1910769_1910767-3,00.html|archive-date=July 18, 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[1994 United States Senate election in Ohio|In 1994]], DeWine ran to succeed Senator [[Howard Metzenbaum]], who was retiring. He defeated prominent attorney [[Joel Hyatt]], Metzenbaum's son-in-law, by 14 percentage points. DeWine was reelected [[2000 United States Senate election in Ohio|in 2000]], defeating businessman [[Ted Celeste]] (former Ohio governor [[Dick Celeste]]'s brother) by 24 percentage points. 

DeWine sat at various points on the Senate [[United States Senate Committee on the Judiciary|Judiciary]]; [[United States Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor and Pensions|Health, Education, Labor and Pensions (HELP)]]; and [[United States Senate Select Committee on Intelligence|Select Intelligence]] Committees, and was the first Ohio senator in nearly six decades to serve on the [[United States Senate Committee on Appropriations|Appropriations Committee]].<ref name="DeWineTributes2007"/> He led the Joint House-Senate Intelligence Committee inquiry into U.S. intelligence failures before and after the [[September 11 attacks]].<ref name="DeWineTributes2007"/>

DeWine also co-chaired the Senate Great Lakes Task Force; the Senate Caucus on Missing, Exploited, and Runaway Children; and the Senate Global AIDS Crisis Working Group; and was a member of the Senate Drug Task Force.<ref name="DeWineTributes2007"/> He was the initial sponsor of the [[Drug-Free Century Act]] in 1999 and was a member of the [[Gang of 14]], a bipartisan group of senators that in 2005 made a compromise on judicial nominees.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.govtrack.us/congress/bill.xpd?bill=s106-5|title=Drug-Free Century Act (1999; 106th Congress S. 5) |work=GovTrack.us|access-date=August 7, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120219184323/http://www.govtrack.us/congress/bill.xpd?bill=s106-5|archive-date=February 19, 2012|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Richardson">{{cite web |last1=Richardson |first1=Seth A. |title=Mike DeWine says he's glad he helped save the filibuster. |url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2018/03/mike_dewine_says_hes_glad_he_s.html |website=Cleveland.com |access-date=November 29, 2024 |date=March 23, 2018}}</ref> He voted in favor of the [[Authorization for Use of Military Force Against Iraq Resolution of 2002]], authorizing the use of force against [[Saddam Hussein]].<ref>{{Cite news|title=Senate Roll Call: Iraq Resolution|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/onpolitics/transcripts/senaterollcall_iraq101002.htm|access-date=February 1, 2022|newspaper=Washington Post|archive-date=September 4, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140904094853/http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/onpolitics/transcripts/senaterollcall_iraq101002.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[2006 United States Senate election in Ohio|In 2006]], DeWine ran for a third term and lost to Congressman [[Sherrod Brown]] by over 12 points.<ref>{{Cite web|date=May 24, 2011|title=Democrats Score First Senate Win|url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/democrats-score-first-senate-win/|access-date=January 7, 2022|website= [[CBS News]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110524014608/http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/11/07/politics/main2161152.shtml|archive-date=May 24, 2011|url-status=live}}</ref> Before the election, DeWine's approval rating stood at 38%, making him the second-most unpopular senator in the country behind [[Rick Santorum]] of Pennsylvania, who also lost reelection that year.<ref>{{Cite web |url= http://surveyusa.com/50State2006/100USSenatorApproval061024Net.htm |title= www.surveyusa.com |access-date= October 28, 2006 |archive-date=December 1, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161201050516/http://www.surveyusa.com/50State2006/100USSenatorApproval061024Net.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref> He received 905,644 fewer votes in 2006 than in 2000.<ref>{{cite web| url= http://www.sos.state.oh.us/SOS/elections/electResultsMain/2000ElectionsResults/USSen11072000.aspx| title= About the Office| work= state.oh.us| access-date= August 7, 2015| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110627172926/http://www.sos.state.oh.us/SOS/elections/electResultsMain/2000ElectionsResults/USSen11072000.aspx| archive-date= June 27, 2011| url-status= dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= http://www.sos.state.oh.us/SOS/elections/electResultsMain/2006ElectionsResults/06-1107USSenate.aspx| title= About the Office| work= state.oh.us| access-date= August 7, 2015| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110627172601/http://www.sos.state.oh.us/SOS/elections/electResultsMain/2006ElectionsResults/06-1107USSenate.aspx| archive-date= June 27, 2011| url-status= dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url= http://www.fec.gov/pubrec/fe2000/2000senate.htm |title= Federal Elections 2000: U.S. Senate Results| work= fec.gov| access-date= August 7, 2015| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20170516153445/http://www.fec.gov/pubrec/fe2000/2000senate.htm| archive-date= May 16, 2017| url-status= live}}</ref>

After leaving the Senate, DeWine accepted positions teaching government courses at [[Cedarville University]], [[Ohio Northern University]], and [[Miami University]]. In 2007, he joined the law firm Keating Muething & Klekamp as corporate investigations group co-chair. He also advised the [[John McCain 2008 presidential campaign]] in Ohio.<ref>{{cite news|first=Jonathan|last=Riskind|title=DeWine to start teaching two courses on politics|url=http://www.dispatch.com/news-story.php?story=dispatch/2007/01/10/20070110-C2-02.html|newspaper=[[Columbus Dispatch]]|date=January 10, 2007|access-date=January 10, 2007 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523054635/http://www.dispatch.com/live/contentbe/dispatch/2007/01/10/20070110-C2-02.html |archive-date= May 23, 2011 }}</ref>

==Attorney General of Ohio (2011–2019)==
[[File:Ohio Attorney General Mike DeWine delivers remarks.jpg|thumb|DeWine delivers remarks at the Department of Justice in 2018]]

On July 21, 2009, DeWine announced his candidacy for [[Ohio attorney general]] in the upcoming 2010 election.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Fields|first1=Reginald|title=Mike DeWine will run for Ohio attorney general|url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2009/07/mike_dewine_will_run_for_ohio.html |date=July 21, 2009 |website=cleveland.com|publisher=Advance Ohio|access-date=August 21, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190821005535/https://www.cleveland.com/open/2009/07/mike_dewine_will_run_for_ohio.html|archive-date=August 21, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> He defeated Democratic incumbent [[Richard Cordray]], 48% to 46%.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cleveland.com/politics/index.ssf/2010/11/ohio_attorney_general_race_res.html|title=Mike DeWine defeats Richard Cordray to win Ohio's attorney general race|work=cleveland.com |first1=Michael |last1=Scott |date=November 3, 2010 |access-date=August 7, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150906044323/http://www.cleveland.com/politics/index.ssf/2010/11/ohio_attorney_general_race_res.html|archive-date=September 6, 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> DeWine was reelected in 2014, defeating challenger [[David A. Pepper]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.dispatch.com/article/20141104/news/141109703|title=DeWine wins re-election as Ohio attorney general|date=November 4, 2014|work=The Columbus Dispatch|access-date=August 21, 2019|agency=The Associated Press |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190821011343/https://www.dispatch.com/article/20141104/news/141109703|archive-date=August 21, 2019|url-status=deviated }}</ref> He carried 83 of Ohio's 88 counties.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mikedewine.com/about/|title=Meet Mike DeWine|website=mikedewine.com|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190821011358/https://www.mikedewine.com/about/|archive-date=August 21, 2019|url-status=dead |access-date=August 21, 2019}}</ref> DeWine's stated goal as attorney general was "protecting Ohio families".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kentwired.com/election_2014/article_cad644b0-647e-11e4-96f0-001a4bcf6878.html|title=DeWine vows to work 'to protect Ohio families' in second term as attorney general|date=November 4, 2018|work=KentWired.com|access-date=October 26, 2018|archive-date=August 4, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200804054457/http://www.kentwired.com/election_2014/article_cad644b0-647e-11e4-96f0-001a4bcf6878.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In the [[2012 Republican presidential primary]], DeWine first endorsed [[Tim Pawlenty]], then endorsed [[Mitt Romney]] after Pawlenty dropped out of the race. On February 17, 2012, DeWine announced he was retracting his endorsement of Romney and endorsed former senate colleague [[Rick Santorum]]. DeWine said, "To be elected president, you have to do more than tear down your opponents. You have to give the American people a reason to vote for you, a reason to hope, a reason to believe that under your leadership, America will be better. Rick Santorum has done that. Sadly, Governor Romney has not."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/campaigns/ap-sources-mike-dewine-backing-santorum-presidential-bid-will-endorse-at-ohio-statehouse/2012/02/17/gIQAIhb0JR_story.html|title=Ohio AG Mike DeWine switches backing from Romney to Santorum before GOP presidential primary|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |agency=Associated Press |access-date=September 5, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120218152226/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/campaigns/ap-sources-mike-dewine-backing-santorum-presidential-bid-will-endorse-at-ohio-statehouse/2012/02/17/gIQAIhb0JR_story.html|archive-date=February 18, 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In 2015, DeWine filed a federal lawsuit against a part of the [[Affordable Care Act]] (ACA).<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/courts/2015/01/26/ohio-warren-co-sues-feds-over-obamacare-fees/22354337/ |first1=Rachel |last1=Richardson |title=Ohio, Warren Co. sue feds over Obamacare fee|date=January 26, 2015|work=Cincinnati.com|access-date=August 7, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181015042110/https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/courts/2015/01/26/ohio-warren-co-sues-feds-over-obamacare-fees/22354337/|archive-date=October 15, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url-status=dead |first1=Mary |last1=Wisniewski |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-ohio-obamacare-idUSKBN0KZ23T20150126 |title=Ohio sues over Obamacare taxes on state, local governments |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170819192008/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-ohio-obamacare-idUSKBN0KZ23T20150126|archive-date=August 19, 2017 |website=Reuters |date=January 26, 2015}}</ref> In the suit, he alleged that the ACA's Transitional Reinsurance Program (which imposed a fee "paid by all employers who provide group health insurance in the workplace", which in 2014 was $63 per covered person and in 2015 was $44 per covered person) was unconstitutional as applied to state and local governments.<ref name="Koff">Stephen Koff, "[http://www.cleveland.com/open/index.ssf/2016/01/ohio_loses_its_latest_challeng.html Ohio loses its latest challenge to Obamacare]", ''Cleveland.com'' (January 6, 2016). {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170802043415/http://www.cleveland.com/open/index.ssf/2016/01/ohio_loses_its_latest_challeng.html|date=august 2, 2017}}.</ref> When he filed the suit, DeWine claimed that the fee was "an unprecedented attempt to destroy the balance of authority between the federal government and the states".<ref name="Koff"/> In January 2016, the federal court dismissed DeWine's suit, with U.S. district judge [[Algenon L. Marbley]] holding that the Transitional Reinsurance Program did not violate the Constitution.<ref name="Koff" /> DeWine appealed, but the [[U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit]] affirmed Marbley's dismissal.<ref>{{cite web |url-status=live |url-access=subscription |first1=John |last1=Kennedy |url=https://www.law360.com/articles/893759/aca-program-isn-t-an-improper-tax-on-states-6th-circ-says |title=ACA Program Isn't An Improper Tax On States, 6th Circ. Says |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170802041412/https://www.law360.com/articles/893759/aca-program-isn-t-an-improper-tax-on-states-6th-circ-says|archive-date=August 2, 2017 |website=Law360 |date=February 17, 2017}}</ref>

As attorney general, DeWine prioritized reducing [[DNA profiling|DNA testing]] turnaround times at the Ohio Bureau of Criminal Investigation, cutting processing times for major criminal investigations from several months to less than one month.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/state/ohio-drops-dna-evidence-testing-turnaround-time-nearly-cuts-testing-time-in-half|title=Ohio drops DNA evidence testing turnaround time; decreased from 125 days to 20 days|date=January 4, 2018|publisher=[[WEWS-TV]] |access-date=October 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181026222712/https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/state/ohio-drops-dna-evidence-testing-turnaround-time-nearly-cuts-testing-time-in-half|archive-date=October 26, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> He also launched a statewide initiative to test nearly 14,000 previously untested [[rape kit]]s, leading to thousands of DNA matches in the [[Combined DNA Index System]] and hundreds of indictments.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.dispatch.com/news/20180223/dna-tests-of-old-rape-kits-identify-up-to-277-suspects-locally|title=DNA tests of old rape kits identify up to 277 suspects locally|date=February 23, 2018|work=Dispatch.com|access-date=October 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181026222500/https://www.dispatch.com/news/20180223/dna-tests-of-old-rape-kits-identify-up-to-277-suspects-locally|archive-date=October 26, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cleveland.com/metro/index.ssf/2018/02/completed_testing_of_13931_rape_kits_signals_progress_unfinished_business_and_investigtions_remain.html|title=Completed testing of 13,931 rape kits signals progress, unfinished business and investigations remain|date=February 23, 2018|work=Cleveland.com|access-date=October 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181026222510/https://www.cleveland.com/metro/index.ssf/2018/02/completed_testing_of_13931_rape_kits_signals_progress_unfinished_business_and_investigtions_remain.html|archive-date=October 26, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> DeWine created the Crimes Against Children Initiative, which combined investigators and prosecutors to pursue child exploitation and abuse cases, and supported task forces targeting [[human trafficking]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.fox19.com/story/16077589/ohio-ag-launches-bold-new-crimes-against-children-initiative/|title=Ohio AG launches Bold New 'Crimes Against Children Initiative'|date=November 18, 2011|publisher=[[WXIX-TV]]|access-date=October 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181026222644/http://www.fox19.com/story/16077589/ohio-ag-launches-bold-new-crimes-against-children-initiative/|archive-date=October 26, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/local-news/oh-cuyahoga/ohio-attorney-general-s-task-force-focuses-on-helping-victims-of-human-trafficking|title=Ohio Attorney General's task force focuses on helping victims of human trafficking|date=January 9, 2018|work=[[WEWS-TV]]|access-date=October 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181026222752/https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/local-news/oh-cuyahoga/ohio-attorney-general-s-task-force-focuses-on-helping-victims-of-human-trafficking|archive-date=October 26, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

DeWine sent drugstore chains letters encouraging them to discontinue the sale of tobacco products.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Harris|first1=Elizabeth|title=States Urge Retail Giants With Pharmacies to Stop Selling Tobacco Products|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/03/17/business/states-urge-retail-giants-with-pharmacies-to-stop-selling-tobacco-products.html |url-access=subscription |access-date=October 12, 2014|newspaper=The New York Times|date=March 16, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141022224006/http://www.nytimes.com/2014/03/17/business/states-urge-retail-giants-with-pharmacies-to-stop-selling-tobacco-products.html?module=Search&mabReward=relbias%3Ar|archive-date=October 22, 2014|url-status=live}}</ref> He targeted prescription drug "[[pill mill]]s" in Ohio that fueled the [[opioid epidemic]] and pursued disciplinary action against doctors and pharmacists accused of improper prescribing practices.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.npr.org/2015/05/19/404184355/how-heroin-made-its-way-from-rural-mexico-to-small-town-america|title=How Heroin Made Its Way From Rural Mexico To Small-Town America|date=May 19, 2015|publisher=NPR|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030035437/https://www.npr.org/2015/05/19/404184355/how-heroin-made-its-way-from-rural-mexico-to-small-town-america|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.springfieldnewssun.com/news/arrests-made-statewide-crackdown-pill-mills/mjkyENTDStDNL5vK5MIXJL/|title=Arrests made in statewide crackdown on 'pill mills'|date=December 20, 2011|work=SpringfieldNewsSun.com|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030090556/https://www.springfieldnewssun.com/news/arrests-made-statewide-crackdown-pill-mills/mjkyENTDStDNL5vK5MIXJL/|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.daytondailynews.com/news/state--regional-govt--politics/ohio-governor-candidates-the-issues-opioid-crisis/8CSq2Msk6FPwf4D1uKAPhM/|title=Ohio governor candidates on the issues: Opioid crisis|date=September 18, 2018|work=Dayton Daily News|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030035742/https://www.daytondailynews.com/news/state--regional-govt--politics/ohio-governor-candidates-the-issues-opioid-crisis/8CSq2Msk6FPwf4D1uKAPhM/|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2013, he established a Heroin Unit to coordinate law enforcement and outreach efforts statewide.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/index.ssf/2013/11/attorney_general_mike_dewine_l.html|title=Attorney General Mike DeWine launches heroin unit to help address rising addiction numbers, death toll|date=November 18, 2013|work=Cleveland.com|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030035414/https://www.cleveland.com/open/index.ssf/2013/11/attorney_general_mike_dewine_l.html|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2017, he announced a broad anti-opioid strategy focused on enforcement, treatment, and prevention.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.toledoblade.com/local/2017/10/31/Ohio-Attorney-General-outlines-recovery-plan.html/|title=Ohio Attorney General outlines opioid recovery plan|date=October 31, 2017|work=ToledoBlade.com|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030035632/https://www.toledoblade.com/local/2017/10/31/Ohio-Attorney-General-outlines-recovery-plan.html/|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> DeWine also sued opioid manufacturers and distributors over their alleged role in the crisis.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/31/us/ohio-sues-pharmaceutical-drug-opioid-epidemic-mike-dewine.html|title=Ohio Sues Drug Makers, Saying They Aided Opioid Epidemic|date=May 31, 2017|work=The New York Times|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181021194831/https://www.nytimes.com/2017/05/31/us/ohio-sues-pharmaceutical-drug-opioid-epidemic-mike-dewine.html|archive-date=October 21, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.dispatch.com/business/20180226/ohio-suing-drug-distributors-over-opioid-epidemic|title=Ohio suing drug distributors over opioid epidemic|date=February 26, 2018|work=Dispatch.com|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030041216/https://www.dispatch.com/business/20180226/ohio-suing-drug-distributors-over-opioid-epidemic|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2018, DeWine sued [[Major League Soccer]] and [[Columbus Crew SC]] owner [[Anthony Precourt]] after Precourt explored moving the team out of the state.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.massivereport.com/2017/10/16/16487076/columbus-crew-sc-reportedly-set-to-move-barring-new-downtown-stadium|title=Columbus Crew SC reportedly set to move barring new downtown stadium|date=October 16, 2018|work=MassiveReport.com|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030035706/https://www.massivereport.com/2017/10/16/16487076/columbus-crew-sc-reportedly-set-to-move-barring-new-downtown-stadium|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sbnation.com/soccer/2017/12/7/16748288/save-the-crew-columbus-ohio-attorney-general-mike-dewine-browns-law|title=Ohio attorney general threatens to invoke law that would keep Crew SC in Columbus|date=December 7, 2018|work=SBNation.com|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030090628/https://www.sbnation.com/soccer/2017/12/7/16748288/save-the-crew-columbus-ohio-attorney-general-mike-dewine-browns-law|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sbnation.com/soccer/2018/3/5/17082248/columbus-crew-sued-ohio-attorney-general-savethecrew-anthony-precourt|title=Ohio sues MLS, Columbus Crew owners to stop the team from relocating|date=March 5, 2018|work=SBNation.com|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030035804/https://www.sbnation.com/soccer/2018/3/5/17082248/columbus-crew-sued-ohio-attorney-general-savethecrew-anthony-precourt|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> After [[Cleveland Browns relocation controversy|the Cleveland Browns moved to Baltimore in the late 1990s]], the [[Ohio General Assembly]] passed a law requiring professional sports teams that had accepted taxpayer assistance to provide an opportunity for local owners to buy the teams before initiating a move.<ref>{{cite act|title=Restrictions on owner of professional sports team that uses a tax-supported facility.|type=Ohio Revised Code §|number=9.67|date=June 20, 1996|url=http://codes.ohio.gov/orc/gp9.67v1|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181027203215/http://codes.ohio.gov/orc/gp9.67v1|archive-date=October 27, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> Later that year, an investor group led by [[Dee Haslam]] and [[Jimmy Haslam]], owners of the [[Cleveland Browns]], and the Edwards family reached an agreement to keep the Crew in Columbus.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cleveland.com/browns/index.ssf/2018/10/the_columbus_crew_had_been_sav.html|title=The Columbus Crew has been saved – by the Haslams and the Art Modell Law|date=October 13, 2018|work=Cleveland.com|access-date=October 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181030035529/https://www.cleveland.com/browns/index.ssf/2018/10/the_columbus_crew_had_been_sav.html|archive-date=October 30, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Governor of Ohio (2019–present)==

===Elections===
{{main|2018 Ohio gubernatorial election|2022 Ohio gubernatorial election}}
[[File:President Trump and Mrs. Trump Arrive in Ohio (48482535896).jpg|thumb|DeWine greeting President [[Donald Trump]] in 2019]]

DeWine announced his candidacy for governor on May 26, 2016, becoming the first major Republican to enter the race to succeed term-limited incumbent [[John Kasich]].<ref name="Governor">{{cite news |url=http://www.whio.com/news/news/breaking-news/dewine-confirms-run-for-governor-in-2018/nrTbf/ |work=WHIO |date=May 26, 2016 |title=Mike DeWine confirms run for Ohio governor in 2018 |first=Will |last=Garbe |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160528103633/http://www.whio.com/news/news/breaking-news/dewine-confirms-run-for-governor-in-2018/nrTbf/ |archive-date=May 28, 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref> He launched his campaign at his annual ice-cream social in [[Cedarville, Ohio]], on June 25, 2017. On December 1, 2017, DeWine selected Ohio secretary of state [[Jon Husted]], who had initially run for governor, as his running mate for lieutenant governor. The move unified much of the state Republican establishment behind the ticket.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2017/12/mike_dewine_picks_jon_husted_a.html |title=Mike DeWine picks Jon Husted as running mate for 2018 governor's race |publisher=Cleveland.com |date=December 1, 2017 }}</ref>

In the Republican primary on May 8, 2018, DeWine defeated incumbent Lieutenant Governor [[Mary Taylor (Ohio politician)|Mary Taylor]], 59.8% to 40.2%.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=831295 |title=Ohio Governor Primary Election 2018 |publisher=Our Campaigns }}</ref> He faced Democratic nominee and former [[Consumer Financial Protection Bureau]] director [[Richard Cordray]] in the November 6 general election, a rematch of their 2010 attorney general race. DeWine defeated Cordray, 50.4% to 46.7%, a difference of about 166,000 votes.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/ohio-governor |title=Ohio Governor Election Results |date=November 17, 2018 |work=The New York Times }}</ref> DeWine was inaugurated as Ohio's 70th governor on January 14, 2019, alongside Lieutenant Governor Husted.

Eligible for a second term, DeWine was challenged for the 2022 Republican nomination due to backlash against his strict [[COVID-19]] restrictions.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theohiopressnetwork.com/news/ohio/opinion-gov-mike-dewine-wants-us-to-forget-his-covid-19-lockdowns-here-s-why/article_3b62ad4a-82d1-11ec-8777-5f5f269536cf.html|title=OPINION: Gov. Mike DeWine wants us to forget his COVID-19 lockdowns; here's why we won't|author=Dilgard, Lad|date=January 31, 2022|website=The Ohio Press Network|access-date=May 4, 2022|archive-date=February 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220213173334/https://www.theohiopressnetwork.com/news/ohio/opinion-gov-mike-dewine-wants-us-to-forget-his-covid-19-lockdowns-here-s-why/article_3b62ad4a-82d1-11ec-8777-5f5f269536cf.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://governor.ohio.gov/media/news-and-media/covid19-update-07222020|title=Governor DeWine Issues Statewide Mask Order, Travel Warning|accessdate=April 14, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wkyc.com/article/news/health/coronavirus/dewine-explains-reason-no-mask-mandate/95-1e6f8bb0-a630-43a7-81be-d76c13fd671d |title=Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine explains why he won't issue mask mandate |publisher=wkyc.com |date=September 14, 2021 |access-date=May 9, 2022}}</ref> He defeated former Congressman [[Jim Renacci]] and farmer Joe Blystone in the primary, and defeated the Democratic nominee, Dayton mayor [[Nan Whaley]], with 62.4% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/11/08/us/elections/results-ohio-governor.html |title=Ohio Governor Election Results 2022 |work=The New York Times |date=November 8, 2022 }}</ref>

===Tenure===
DeWine's governorship has been characterized by a pragmatic, sometimes bipartisan approach that has occasionally put him at odds with the Ohio Republican Party's more conservative wing. Themes have included public-health preparedness, economic recovery after COVID-19, major infrastructure investment, children's issues (especially foster care and lead poisoning), environmental initiatives like the H2Ohio water-quality program, opioid and human trafficking prevention, education reform, workforce development, and a cautious approach to social issues.

====First term (2019–2023)====
{{see also|COVID-19 pandemic in Ohio}}
[[File:President Joe Biden with Ohio Governor Mike DeWine.jpg|thumb|Governor DeWine discussing the [[COVID-19]] response with President [[Joe Biden]] in 2021]] 

DeWine was inaugurated on January 14, 2019. On his first day in office, he signed six executive orders establishing priorities: the Governor's Children's Initiative (foster care and infant mortality), disability inclusion, mental health prevention, anti-discrimination in state employment (including LGBTQ protections), and the RecoveryOhio Initiative to combat the opioid crisis through prevention, treatment, and recovery.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/g66l-2019/01/589560b6865486/what-to-know-about-gov-mike-de.html |title=What to know about Gov. Mike DeWine's first six executive orders |date=January 15, 2019 |publisher=Cleveland.com }}</ref> In February, President [[Donald Trump]] appointed DeWine to the bipartisan [[Council of Governors]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theday.com/article/20190222/NWS12/190229834 |title=Trump appoints Lamont to governors council |website=The Day |date=February 22, 2019 }}</ref> In March, DeWine delivered his first State of the State address, proposing a gas tax increase for infrastructure and investments in children's services, mental health, and water quality.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cleveland.com/politics/2019/03/gov-mike-dewine-proposes-more-money-for-roads-children-in-state-of-the-state-speech.html |title=Gov. Mike DeWine proposes more money for roads, children in State of the State speech |date=March 5, 2019 |publisher=Cleveland.com }}</ref>

In April 2019, DeWine signed the [[Heartbeat Bill]], prohibiting abortions after a fetal heartbeat is detected.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.npr.org/2019/04/11/712455980/a-bill-banning-most-abortions-becomes-law-in-ohio |title=A Bill Banning Most Abortions Becomes Law In Ohio |date=April 11, 2019 |work=NPR }}</ref> In July, he signed the biennial budget (gas tax increase, school choice expansion) and House Bill 6, a controversial energy bill subsidizing nuclear and coal plants, later linked to a major bribery scandal involving FirstEnergy.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.news5cleveland.com/news/politics/ohio-politics/new-texts-show-firstenergy-allegedly-working-with-gov-dewine-to-pass-house-bill-6 |title=New texts show FirstEnergy allegedly working with Gov. DeWine to pass House Bill 6 |date=June 18, 2024 |publisher=News5Cleveland }}</ref> In August, after the [[2019 Dayton shooting|Dayton mass shooting]], DeWine proposed a 17-point gun safety plan including red-flag laws.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.npr.org/2019/08/06/748805187/ohio-governor-proposes-new-gun-control-laws-marking-shift-from-past-gop-leadersh |title=Ohio governor proposes new gun control laws, marking shift from past GOP leadership |date=August 6, 2019 |work=NPR }}</ref> In November, he launched H2Ohio for water quality improvement.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wosu.org/news/2019-11-14/dewine-pours-out-details-of-h2ohio-water-quality-program |title=DeWine Pours Out Details Of H2Ohio Water Quality Program |date=November 14, 2019 |publisher=WOSU }}</ref> In December, he supported local plastic bag bans and lead exposure initiatives.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2019/12/gov-mike-dewine-favors-letting-ohio-cities-ban-plastic-bags.html |title=Gov. Mike DeWine favors letting Ohio cities ban plastic bags |date=December 4, 2019 |publisher=Cleveland.com }}</ref>

DeWine's early, aggressive response to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] drew national praise and backlash. In January and February 2020, DeWine began monitoring emerging cases. In March, he limited the Arnold Sports Festival, banned large gatherings, closed schools statewide, closed dine-in services, postponed a primary election, and issued a stay-at-home order.<ref name="Witte"/> Gradual reopening began in May, as DeWine had daily briefings with [[Ohio Department of Health]] Director [[Amy Acton]], who resigned in June amid threats. In July, DeWine imposed a statewide mask mandate and briefly banned hydroxychloroquine for COVID treatment before reversing the ban.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.10tv.com/article/news/local/ohio/dewine-asks-ohio-pharmacy-board-to-reverse-decision-prohibiting-hydroxychloroquine-for-treating-covid-19/530-48a1b374-97a0-47a4-a737-a2d83bddec0e |title=Ohio pharmacy board withdraws rule prohibiting use of hydroxychloroquine to treat COVID-19 |date=July 30, 2020 |publisher=10TV }}</ref> In May 2021, DeWine's "Vax-a-Million" lottery boosted COVID vaccination uptake. In July, he signed a biennial budget with broadband, Appalachian, and water investments.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/13/us/ohio-vax-a-million-covid-vaccine-lottery.html |title=Ohio Announces Lottery for Vaccinated Adults and Children |date=May 13, 2021 |work=The New York Times}}</ref> Also in 2021, he signed criminal justice reforms, including ending cash bail for misdemeanors.

In 2022, DeWine signed a stand-your-ground law. Redistricting was contentious: multiple maps were struck down for gerrymandering, and DeWine supported gerrymandered maps despite criticism.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2022/05/gov-mike-dewine-says-ohios-new-statehouse-maps-are-unconstitutional-but-votes-for-them-anyway.html |title=Gov. Mike DeWine says Ohio's new Statehouse maps are unconstitutional, but votes for them anyway |date=May 5, 2022 |publisher=Cleveland.com }}</ref> In January 2022, Intel announced a major semiconductor campus. DeWine awarded grants for human trafficking prevention and survivor services.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.americantowns.com/news/governor-dewine-awards-over-13-million-in-grants-for-human-trafficking-prevention-programs-31315083-columbus-oh.html |title=Governor DeWine Awards Over $1.3 Million in Grants for Human Trafficking Prevention Programs |date=March 4, 2022 }}</ref>

====Second term (2023–present)====
On inauguration day 2023, DeWine signed executive orders including anti-discrimination and skills-based hiring.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wkbn.com/news/local-news/gov-dewine-signs-5-new-executive-orders/ |title=Gov. DeWine signs 5 new executive orders |date=January 9, 2023 |publisher=WKBN }}</ref> In February, a Norfolk Southern train derailed in East Palestine; DeWine activated the National Guard and supported rail safety.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://apnews.com/article/ohio-train-derailment-east-palestine-dewine-16b0d4199e2e1f2f95e9e83c8c01088f |title=Ohio Gov. DeWine urges Congress to pass rail safety bill after East Palestine derailment |date=March 8, 2023 |publisher=Associated Press }}</ref> Also in 2023, DeWine signed the "Backpack Bill" expanding vouchers. In December, he vetoed House Bill 68, which prohibited [[Transgender health care|gender-affirming care]] for minors; his veto was overridden in January 2024.

DeWine issued executive orders banning gender-affirming surgery for minors and restricting care.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2024/01/ohio-legislature-overrides-gov-mike-dewines-veto-of-bill-banning-transgender-athletes-gender-affirming-care-for-minors.html |title=Ohio legislature overrides Gov. Mike DeWine's veto of bill banning transgender athletes, gender-affirming care for minors |date=January 24, 2024 |publisher=Cleveland.com }}</ref> In 2024, ground was broken on the Intel campus and H2Ohio expanded. Amid an influx of Haitian migrants, DeWine deployed state troopers and funding to Springfield.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/gop-gov-dewine-defends-haitian-immigrants-springfield-work/story?id=113690058 |title=GOP Gov. DeWine defends Haitian immigrants: 'They came to Springfield to work' |date=September 15, 2024 |publisher=ABC News }}</ref>

In 2025, DeWine formed the Property Tax Working Group<ref>{{cite news |url=https://ohiocpa.com/for-the-public/news/2025/10/03/ohio-property-tax-working-group-finalizes-20-reform-proposals |title=Ohio Property Tax Working Group finalizes 20 reform proposals |date=October 3, 2025 }}</ref> and issued an executive order that state employees return to work in person. In July, he signed a $60 billion budget. In August, he deployed the National Guard to Washington, D.C. In December, he announced $65.6 million for Welcome Home Ohio housing.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://spectrumnews1.com/oh/columbus/news/2025/12/09/ohio-housing-access-program |title=Gov. DeWine aims to improve access to housing across Ohio |date=December 9, 2025 |publisher=Spectrum News }}</ref> In January 2026, he announced that he intended to make a statement on the death penalty, on which Ohio has had a ''de facto'' moratorium since 2019, but he has not yet done so.<ref name=":1">{{cite news |url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/2025/12/gov-mike-dewine-says-hell-make-announcement-on-ohios-death-penalty-in-january.html |title=Gov. Mike DeWine says he'll make announcement on Ohio's death penalty in January |date=December 11, 2025 |publisher=Cleveland.com }}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Today in Ohio |date=March 11, 2026 |title=Mike DeWine had a chance to make history on the death penalty. He didn't take it |url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/03/mike-dewine-had-a-chance-to-make-history-on-the-death-penalty-he-didnt-take-it.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260430071009/https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/03/mike-dewine-had-a-chance-to-make-history-on-the-death-penalty-he-didnt-take-it.html |archive-date=April 30, 2026 |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=Cleveland.com |language=en}}</ref> In April 2026, DeWine announced he would make an official statement about his personal feelings about the death penalty during the week after Ohio's gubernatorial primaries, which took place on May 5.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jeremy Pelzer |first=cleveland com |date=April 14, 2026 |title=Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine sets new date to reveal his personal stance on state's death penalty |url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/04/ohio-gov-mike-dewine-sets-new-date-to-reveal-his-personal-stance-on-states-death-penalty.html |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=cleveland |language=en}}</ref>

==Political positions==

===Abortion===
[[File:President George W. Bush congratulates Senator Mike DeWine, R-Ohio, on S. 650, the Pediatric Equity Research Act of 2003.jpg|thumb|President [[George W. Bush]] congratulates Senator Mike DeWine on the passing of the Pediatric Equity Research Act of 2003]]
In April 2019, DeWine signed House Bill 493, known as the [[Ohio "Heartbeat Bill"]], into law, prohibiting abortion after a heartbeat is detected in a fetus, with no exceptions for cases of rape and incest, imposing one of the nation's most extensive abortion restrictions.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://time.com/5568922/ohio-ban-abortion-first-heartbeat/|title=&#124; Time|access-date=April 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190412011849/http://time.com/5568922/ohio-ban-abortion-first-heartbeat/|archive-date=April 12, 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2019/04/11/ohio-governor-to-sign-ban-on-abortion-after-first-detectable-heartbeat.html|title=Ohio governor to sign ban on abortion after first detectable heartbeat|date=April 11, 2019|publisher=CNBC|access-date=April 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190412135856/https://www.cnbc.com/2019/04/11/ohio-governor-to-sign-ban-on-abortion-after-first-detectable-heartbeat.html|archive-date=April 12, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> DeWine [[Opposition to the legalization of abortion|opposes abortion]]. In the Senate, he was the lead sponsor of the [[Unborn Victims of Violence Act]] and voted for the [[Partial Birth Abortion Ban Act]] in 2003.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1081/vote_108_1_00051.htm | title=U.S. Senate: U.S. Senate Roll Call Votes 108th Congress – 1st Session }}</ref><ref>See S. 1019 (Unborn Victims of Violence Act), introduced May 7, 2003; S. 146 (Unborn Victims of Violence Act of 2003), introduced January 13, 2003; S.480 (Unborn Victims of Violence Act of 2001), introduced January 7, 2001. See also Karen MacPherson, "[http://www.toledoblade.com/frontpage/2004/03/26/Senate-votes-to-outlaw-harming-the-unborn-abortion-activists-fear-women-s-rights-eroded.html Senate votes to outlaw harming the unborn; abortion activists fear women's rights eroded] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180321064752/http://www.toledoblade.com/frontpage/2004/03/26/Senate-votes-to-outlaw-harming-the-unborn-abortion-activists-fear-women-s-rights-eroded.html|date=March 21, 2018}}" (March 26, 2004), ''Toledo Blade''; [[Carl Hulse]], "[https://www.nytimes.com/2004/03/26/us/senate-outlaws-injury-to-fetus-during-a-crime.html Senate Outlaws Injury to Fetus During a Crime] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180226073753/http://www.nytimes.com/2004/03/26/us/senate-outlaws-injury-to-fetus-during-a-crime.html|date=February 26, 2018}}" (March 26, 2004), ''The New York Times''; Sheryl Gay Stolberg, "[https://www.nytimes.com/2003/05/08/us/washington-talk-from-cnn-to-congress-legislation-by-anecdote.html Washington Talk: From CNN to Congress, Legislation by Anecdote] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171229145225/http://www.nytimes.com/2003/05/08/us/washington-talk-from-cnn-to-congress-legislation-by-anecdote.html|date=December 29, 2017}}" (May 8, 2003), ''The New York Times''.</ref> In December 2020, DeWine signed a bill that said "fetal remains from surgical abortions in Ohio must be cremated or buried"; failure to do so would be a misdemeanor of the first degree.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://pittsburgh.cbslocal.com/2020/12/30/ohio-governor-signs-bill-requiring-burial-of-fetal-remains/|title=Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine Signs Bill Requiring Fetal Remains From Surgical Abortions Be Cremated Or Buried|website=[[KDKA-TV]]|date=December 30, 2020|access-date=January 1, 2021|archive-date=January 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210101174226/https://pittsburgh.cbslocal.com/2020/12/30/ohio-governor-signs-bill-requiring-burial-of-fetal-remains/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Choi|first=Joseph|date=December 31, 2020|title=Ohio governor signs bill requiring women to choose burial or cremation of aborted fetal tissue|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/532259-ohio-governor-signs-bill-requiring-women-to-choose-burial-or-cremation|access-date=January 1, 2021|website=The Hill|archive-date=January 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210101000916/https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/532259-ohio-governor-signs-bill-requiring-women-to-choose-burial-or-cremation|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[November 2023 Ohio Issue 1]] overturned Ohio's abortion ban.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Filer |first1=Christine |last2=Langer |first2=Gary |last3=De Jong |first3=Allison |date=November 7, 2023 |title=Ohio voters broadly support abortion access, early exit poll of ballot measure shows |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/ohio-voters-broadly-support-abortion-access-preliminary-exit/story?id=104696547 |access-date=November 8, 2023 |website=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] |language=en}}</ref>

===Capital punishment===
A leader of Ohio's ACLU chapter, which opposes capital punishment, has called the death penalty "a legacy issue" for DeWine.<ref name=":5" /> As a state senator in the 1980s, DeWine helped write the legislation that reinstated Ohio's death penalty following a [[Furman v. Georgia|nationwide moratorium]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |last=Ludlow |first=Randy |title=Described as a death penalty supporter, Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine responds 'I didn't say that' |url=https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/healthcare/2020/12/16/ohio-gov-mike-dewine-no-longer-supports-capital-punishment-threatens-veto-of-stand-your-ground-bill/6505426002/ |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=The Columbus Dispatch |language=en-US}}</ref> Although Catholic, DeWine did not join the Pope and Catholic bishops in openly opposing the death penalty; as governor, he avoided stating his views on the capital punishment for most of his tenure.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2020/02/why-wont-gov-mike-dewine-say-whether-he-supports-ohios-death-penalty.html|title=Why won't Gov. Mike DeWine say whether he supports Ohio's death penalty?|date=February 19, 2020|website=Cleveland.com|access-date=October 8, 2020|archive-date=December 17, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201217024954/https://www.cleveland.com/open/2020/02/why-wont-gov-mike-dewine-say-whether-he-supports-ohios-death-penalty.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In late May 2026, DeWine endorsed Republican gubernatorial candidate [[Vivek Ramaswamy]]; Ramaswamy supports capital punishment "only in the most egregious cases," while his opponent, Democratic gubernatorial candidate [[Amy Acton]], said she would continue DeWine's moratorium on capital punishment.<ref name=":6" /><ref name=":9" />

On June 16, 2026, DeWine voiced support for abolishing the death penalty in Ohio; this stance differs from that of most other Republicans.<ref name=":7">{{Cite web |last=Osgood |first=Brian |date= |title=Ohio Governor Mike DeWine opposes death penalty, reversing past support |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/6/16/ohio-governor-mike-dewine-opposes-death-penalty-reversing-past-support |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260617093457/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/6/16/ohio-governor-mike-dewine-opposes-death-penalty-reversing-past-support |archive-date=June 17, 2026 |access-date=June 17, 2026 |website=[[Al Jazeera]] |language=en}}</ref> It also differs from DeWine's previous stance on the death penalty, as he was once a proponent of capital punishment. In a statement on June 16, 2026, DeWine said he no longer believes the death penalty serves as a deterrent to murder and that the "moral justification I had for voting for the death penalty simply no longer exists". He discussed the harmful effect the decades-long delays in administering the death penalty had on murder victims' families, as well as the harmful psychological effects prison workers experienced from participating in executions, particularly botched ones. He called on state legislators to abolish the death penalty and also noted that voters had the power to amend the state constitution with ballot measures.<ref name="Smyth2026">{{Cite web |last1=Smyth |first1=Julie Carr |last2=Aftoora-Orsagos |first2=Patrick |date=June 16, 2026 |title=Republican Gov. Mike DeWine wants Ohio to abolish the death penalty, saying it is not a deterrent |url=https://apnews.com/article/death-penalty-ohio-dewine-6210d7fbcecde9fe88657a76521e90fe |access-date=June 16, 2026 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":8">{{Cite news |last=Bellware |first=Kim |date=June 16, 2026 |title=Ohio Republican governor urges end to death penalty, saying it cannot be morally justified |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jun/16/ohio-governor-death-penalty-mike-dewine |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260617092657/https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jun/16/ohio-governor-death-penalty-mike-dewine |archive-date=June 17, 2026 |access-date=June 17, 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref name=":7" /> Several Ohio lawmakers and candidates reacted to DeWine's proposal to end the death penalty in Ohio, with Ramaswamy and Ohio Attorney General candidate [[Keith Faber]] expressing their opposition with DeWine's abolitionist stance. Several other politicians and figures, including some Republicans, Ohio's former DRC director, and Democratic state senator [[Nickie Antonio]], expressed their support of DeWine's stance and acknowledged the evolution his position had undergone over the years.<ref name="Smyth2026"/><ref>{{Cite web|title=Ohio lawmakers, candidates react to DeWine's call to end the death penalty|url=https://www.10tv.com/article/news/local/ohio/mike-dewine-death-penalty-ohio-reaction/530-9b60cbbb-9e98-48ac-970c-e638873a6ce3|date=June 16, 2026|website=10TV}}</ref>

No executions have been conducted in Ohio since DeWine took office, and he stated he had delayed all executions due to "ongoing problems involving the willingness of pharmaceutical suppliers to provide drugs to the Ohio Department of Rehabilitation and Correction (DRC)"; before he announced his opposition to capital punishment, he said he was postponing executions in the interest of ensuring non-condemned Ohioans can use state-run programs to access the same drugs as those in Ohio's lethal injection cocktail.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dietz |first=Matthew |date=June 20, 2025 |title=Ohio Gov. DeWine issues reprieves of execution for 3 inmates on death row |url=https://www.wlwt.com/article/ohio-mike-dewine-governor-execution-reprieve-death-row/65133652 |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=WLWT |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last=Pelzer |first=Jeremy |date=January 30, 2026 |title=There's a big impediment to Ohio resuming executions, even if it wanted to, DeWine says |url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/01/theres-a-big-impediment-to-ohio-resuming-executions-even-if-it-wanted-to-dewine-says.html |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=cleveland.com |language=en}}</ref> DeWine proposed that if his successors want to reinstate Ohio's death penalty, they should consider passing a "shield law" to keep pharmaceutical suppliers from finding out that the DRC used their drugs for executions.<ref name=":4" /> Every time an execution date has been scheduled during DeWine's tenure as governor, he has granted executive clemency postponing it for several years.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/stories/news-brief-new-set-of-reprieves-push-back-first-three-ohio-executions-of-2021-until-2023|title=News Brief – New Set of Reprieves Push Back First Three Ohio Executions of 2021 Until 2023|publisher=Death Penalty Information Center|access-date=October 7, 2020|archive-date=October 9, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201009105133/https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/stories/news-brief-new-set-of-reprieves-push-back-first-three-ohio-executions-of-2021-until-2023|url-status=live}}</ref> Ohio has thus had a ''de facto'' death penalty moratorium since 2019.<ref name=":1" />

In 2020, a reporter asked DeWine a question that implied DeWine was a death penalty proponent, and DeWine responded, "I didn't say that. I said I am following the law in Ohio [regarding capital punishment]."<ref name=":3" /> In the same answer, he implied that he disagreed with the common moral justification for capital punishment that it serves as a deterrent, considering that it takes decades for Ohio death row inmates to complete their appeals. DeWine also proposed that lawmakers legalize other methods of execution, but declined to offer suggestions. He said the death penalty was not a priority for the state, adding, "We're not going to execute anyone under the status quo."<ref name=":3" /> In December 2024, DeWine told a crowd of reporters, "We've not had any executions since I've been governor. We will not as long as I'm governor."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sarat |first=Austin |date=April 20, 2026 |title=Time is running out for Gov. DeWine to take a side on the death penalty |url=https://thehill.com/opinion/criminal-justice/5836996-governor-dewine-death-penalty/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260430074016/https://thehill.com/opinion/criminal-justice/5836996-governor-dewine-death-penalty/ |archive-date=April 30, 2026 |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=The Hill}}</ref>

In late 2025, DeWine announced that he would use part of his final [[State of the State address]] to give his personal position on capital punishment; he said he would write his statement over Christmas and would have something to say the next month. But in January 2026, a spokesman said that DeWine had "no new public statements beyond his previous comments."<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Downing |first=Andy |date=March 11, 2026 |title=The death penalty in Ohio stands at a critical juncture |url=https://matternews.org/community/the-death-penalty-in-ohio-stands-at-a-critical-juncture/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260430073445/https://matternews.org/community/the-death-penalty-in-ohio-stands-at-a-critical-juncture/ |archive-date=April 30, 2026 |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=Matter News |language=en-US}}</ref> Reporters previously speculated that DeWine would use the address to announce that he did not support the death penalty.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jeremy Pelzer |first=cleveland com |date=March 9, 2026 |title=Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine set to deliver final State of the State address; What to expect |url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/03/ohio-gov-mike-dewine-set-to-deliver-final-state-of-the-state-address-what-to-expect.html |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=cleveland |language=en}}</ref> His March 2026 State of the State address did not mention the subject.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Ohio public defender calls for death penalty abolishment as moratorium continues |url=https://www.toledoblade.com/local/politics/2026/03/21/ohio-public-defender-calls-death-penalty-abolishment-moratorium-continues/stories/20260320107 |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=The Blade |language=en}}</ref> In a May 2026 forum discussing his endorsement of Ramaswamy and other issues, DeWine said he found the response to his upcoming statement overblown, but also confirmed he would deliver his remarks soon: "I don't want to build this up because I'm going to do it shortly, and I think there's been a lot of hype about this, but there really shouldn't be. It'll simply be Mike DeWine reflecting on the death penalty."<ref name=":9">{{Cite web |last=Henry |first=Megan |date=May 26, 2026 |title=Ohio Gov. DeWine talks endorsing Ramaswamy, why legalizing sports betting is his 'biggest mistake' |url=https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/05/26/ohio-gov-dewine-talks-endorsing-ramaswamy-why-legalizing-sports-betting-is-his-biggest-mistake/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260528163312/https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2026/05/26/ohio-gov-dewine-talks-endorsing-ramaswamy-why-legalizing-sports-betting-is-his-biggest-mistake/ |archive-date=May 28, 2026 |access-date=May 28, 2026 |website=[[Ohio Capital Journal]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":6">{{Cite web |last=Pelzer |first=Jeremy |date=May 17, 2026 |title=Ohio's death penalty: Where Amy Acton and Vivek Ramaswamy stand on resuming executions |url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/05/ohios-death-penalty-where-amy-acton-and-vivek-ramaswamy-stand-on-resuming-executions.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260528163738/https://www.cleveland.com/news/2026/05/ohios-death-penalty-where-amy-acton-and-vivek-ramaswamy-stand-on-resuming-executions.html |archive-date=May 28, 2026 |access-date=May 28, 2026 |website=Cleveland.com |language=en}}</ref>

In 2025 and 2026, fellow Republican lawmakers in Ohio, including Attorney General [[Dave Yost]] and House Speaker [[Matt Huffman]], expressed concern that DeWine might commute all death sentences before he leaves office in 2027. Yost and Huffman also confirmed that the predominantly Republican Ohio state legislature largely supports the death penalty and would not cooperate with any legislative efforts to abolish it. In his June 2026 statement announcing his opposition to the death penalty, DeWine noted that he and Huffman had discussed their opposing stances on the issue.<ref name=":7" /> Meanwhile, death penalty abolitionists have said they want DeWine to commute all the state's death sentences to [[life without parole]], which would allow him to circumvent legislative resistance to ending capital punishment.<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=WSYX |date=December 16, 2025 |title=Ohio attorney general concerned over governor's potential death penalty announcement |url=https://abc6onyourside.com/news/local/ohio-attorney-general-dave-yost-concerned-over-governors-mike-dewine-potential-death-penalty-announcement-capital-punishment |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=WSYX |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Two Reports from Ohio Draw Starkly Different Conclusions about the Future of the State's Death Penalty System |url=https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/two-reports-from-ohio-draw-starkly-different-conclusions-about-the-future-of-the-states-death-penalty-system |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260430073012/https://deathpenaltyinfo.org/two-reports-from-ohio-draw-starkly-different-conclusions-about-the-future-of-the-states-death-penalty-system |archive-date=April 30, 2026 |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=[[Death Penalty Information Center]] |language=en-US}}</ref> In December 2019, DeWine said he believed the future of capital punishment should be up to citizen voters and legislators.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gov. DeWine looks back at 2019's successes and 'unfinished business' |url=https://www.toledoblade.com/local/politics/2019/12/30/ohio-governor-dewine-looks-back-2019-successes-unfinished-business/stories/20191226082 |access-date=April 30, 2026 |website=The Blade |language=en}}</ref> Death penalty supporters have criticized DeWine's moratorium as expensive to uphold and denying closure to victims' families, while death penalty opponents have encouraged DeWine to abolish capital punishment due to [[Capital punishment debate in the United States#Racial and gender factors|racial discrimination]], innocence cases, and geographical arbitrariness. {{As of|2026}}, there are over 108 inmates on Ohio's death row, many of whom have received reprieves from DeWine. DeWine has not said whether he will commute all active death sentences before leaving office.<ref name=":6" /><ref name=":8" />

===Gerrymandering===
In 2021, DeWine signed a redistricting map that favored Republicans. The map gave Republicans an advantage in 12 out of 15 districts, leaving two safely Democratic districts and one toss-up district. The map passed the Ohio legislature without any support from Democrats. Voting rights advocates called on DeWine to veto the pro-Republican redistricting map. In 2018, Ohio voted in a referendum for anti-gerrymandering reform that encouraged bipartisan support for redistricting maps. The same year, DeWine pledged to honor the voters' wishes and support a redistricting process conducted in a bipartisan way, but in 2021 he approved the changes for 2022 onward.<ref>{{Cite web|last1=Tobias|first1=Andrew J.|date=November 20, 2021|title=Gov. Mike DeWine approves Ohio congressional map bill that likely strengthens GOP share|url=https://www.cleveland.com/news/2021/11/gov-mike-dewine-approves-ohio-congressional-map-bill-that-strengthens-gop-share.html|access-date=November 20, 2021|website=cleveland|language=en|archive-date=November 20, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211120152043/https://www.cleveland.com/news/2021/11/gov-mike-dewine-approves-ohio-congressional-map-bill-that-strengthens-gop-share.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Gun control===
[[File:After Gov Dewine Spoke.webm|thumb|Ohio governor Mike DeWine leaves the stage after speaking, and the crowd shouts "Do something!" in reaction to the [[2019 Dayton shooting]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.npr.org/2019/08/05/748239149/do-something-calls-ring-out-after-mass-shootings-in-el-paso-and-dayton|title='Do Something!': Calls For Action After Mass Shootings In El Paso And Dayton|website=NPR|date=August 5, 2019|access-date=August 6, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190806205156/https://www.npr.org/2019/08/05/748239149/do-something-calls-ring-out-after-mass-shootings-in-el-paso-and-dayton|archive-date=August 6, 2019|url-status=live|last1=Chappell|first1=Bill}}</ref>]]
In Congress, DeWine was known for his support for gun control measures. In 2004, he cosponsored an amendment to renew the [[Federal Assault Weapons Ban]]. He has repeatedly received an "F" rating from the National Rifle Association.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.cnn.com/2004/ALLPOLITICS/02/25/senate.guns.ap/index.html|title=Senate considers protecting gunmakers|publisher=Associated Press|date = February 25, 2004|access-date=March 27, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050326064431/http://cnn.com/2004/ALLPOLITICS/02/25/senate.guns.ap/index.html|archive-date=March 26, 2005}}</ref> The National Rifle Association endorsed him for governor.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nraila.org/articles/20180926/nra-endorses-mike-dewine-for-governor-jon-husted-for-lieutenant-governor|title=NRA Endorses Mike DeWine for Governor, Jon Husted for Lieutenant Governor|publisher=NRA-ILA|date=September 26, 2018|access-date=August 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190804154111/https://www.nraila.org/articles/20180926/nra-endorses-mike-dewine-for-governor-jon-husted-for-lieutenant-governor|archive-date=August 4, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> DeWine was one of only two Republican senators to vote against the [[Protection of Lawful Commerce in Arms Act]], which banned lawsuits against gun manufacturers, distributors and dealers for criminal misuse of their products. In the 2006 election cycle, DeWine was the first senatorial candidate to be endorsed by the Brady Campaign to Prevent Gun Violence; he displayed the endorsement on his campaign webpage.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nraila.org/Legislation/Federal/Read.aspx?id=2291|title=Brady Campaign Endorses DeWine|access-date=June 23, 2006|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101110114427/http://www.nraila.org/Legislation/Federal/Read.aspx?id=2291|archive-date=November 10, 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=109&session=1&vote=00219|title=U.S. Senate Roll Call Votes 109th Congress – 1st Session – Vote Summary on Passage of S. 397, As Amended|publisher=U.S. Senate|date=July 29, 2005|access-date=March 27, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070330060959/http://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=109&session=1&vote=00219|archive-date=March 30, 2007|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2019, DeWine proposed a [[red flag law]] for Ohio that would allow courts to take guns from people seen as a threats to others or themselves. In January 2021, he signed into law a bill that removed the requirement to attempt to retreat before shooting in self-defense, and in March 2022 he signed a bill into law removing the requirement to possess a license to carry a concealed handgun in public.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/ohio-gov-mike-dewine-signs-bill-allowing-permitless-concealed-carry-rcna20156 | title=Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine signs bill allowing permitless concealed carry | website=[[NBC News]] | date=March 15, 2022 | access-date=December 20, 2023 | archive-date=December 20, 2023 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231220172009/https://www.nbcnews.com/news/amp/rcna20156 | url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.statenews.org/government-politics/2021-01-05/dewine-signs-stand-your-ground-bill-into-law | title=DeWine Signs 'Stand Your Ground' Bill into Law | date=January 5, 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Balmert|first1=Jessie|title=Ohio Gov. DeWine proposes 'red flag' law, expanding background checks for gun sales|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2019/08/06/dayton-ohio-shooting-governor-mike-dewine-gun-changes/1930220001/|work=USA Today|access-date=August 27, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190827191706/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2019/08/06/dayton-ohio-shooting-governor-mike-dewine-gun-changes/1930220001/|archive-date=August 27, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Highway safety===
As U.S. senator, DeWine joined a bipartisan effort to lower the national maximum blood-alcohol limit from .1% to .08% and to require reporting of vehicle-related deaths on private property like parking lots and driveways.<ref name="US Today August 9, 2005">{{cite news|title=Ohio senator makes his mark on highway safety|date=August 9, 2005|url=http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/money/autos/2005-08-09-dewine-usat_x.htm|quote=...drunken driving [is] a central focus of DeWine's highway-safety attention. He was behind the move to make 0.08% the national maximum blood-alcohol limit, which it became this month when Minnesota was the final state to adopt it... DeWine says his years in politics helped persuade him to do something about the injuries and deaths that don't occur on public property, which is what regulators previously focused on. He wanted data about incidents in parking lots and driveways to be routinely collected, too.|access-date=October 2, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006192923/http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/money/autos/2005-08-09-dewine-usat_x.htm|archive-date=October 6, 2014|url-status=live}}</ref> He sponsored legislation on determining when aging tires become unsafe.<ref name="Tire Business Jan 23, 2004">{{cite web|title=Sen. DeWine introduces tire aging bill|date=January 23, 2004|url=http://www.tirebusiness.com/article/20040123/NEWS/301239999&template=printart|quote=WASHINGTON (Jan. 23, 2004) – Sen. Mike DeWine, R-Ohio, has introduced a package of five highway safety bills, including one requiring tire retailers to disclose the month and year in which the tires they sell are produced. Mr. DeWine's bill also would require the National Academy of Sciences to do a definitive study of how both used and unused tires age{{snd}}with an eye toward discovering the point at which an aged tire becomes unsafe.|access-date=October 2, 2014}}</ref>

===LGBTQ+ rights===
DeWine opposes [[same-sex marriage]] and as a U.S. senator voted for the [[Defense of Marriage Act]], which bars federal recognition of same-sex marriage and allows states to refuse to recognize such marriages performed in other states,<ref>{{cite web | title=U.S. Senate: U.S. Senate Roll Call Votes 104th Congress - 2nd Session | url=https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_lists/roll_call_vote_cfm.cfm?congress=104&session=2&vote=00280 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://eu.cincinnati.com/story/news/politics/2015/04/25/gay-marriage-case-born-divided-ohio/26261633/|title=How gay-marriage case was born of a divided Ohio|work=Cincinnati.com|date=April 25, 2015|access-date=September 21, 2018|archive-date=March 15, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230315075753/https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/politics/2015/04/25/gay-marriage-case-born-divided-ohio/26261633/|url-status=live}}</ref> and sponsored the [[Federal Marriage Amendment]] to the U.S. Constitution, which would have banned same-sex marriage.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.mcclatchydc.com/latest-news/article24454828.html|title=Ohio is pivotal to Democratic hopes to reclaim the Senate|work=mcclatchydc|access-date=September 21, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180921153317/https://www.mcclatchydc.com/latest-news/article24454828.html|archive-date=September 21, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> He argued in the Supreme Court in favor of bans on same-sex marriage, saying that such bans infringe on "no fundamental right" and that states should not have to recognize same-sex couples who married in other states. DeWine was the attorney general against [[Jim Obergefell]] in the case ''[[Obergefell v. Hodges]]''. The Supreme Court issued a landmark ruling against DeWine and other defendants, finding same-sex marriage bans unconstitutional.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/index.ssf/2015/06/attorney_general_mike_dewine_g.html|title=Attorney General Mike DeWine, Gov. John Kasich acknowledge gay marriage ruling is law in Ohio|work=cleveland.com|access-date=September 21, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180921190950/https://www.cleveland.com/open/index.ssf/2015/06/attorney_general_mike_dewine_g.html|archive-date=September 21, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/politics/2015/03/27/dewine-defends-state-s-ban/23363247007/|title=DeWine defends state's ban on same-sex marriage|last=Torry|first=Jack|newspaper=The Columbus Dispatch|date=March 27, 2015|access-date=September 21, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180921153211/http://www.dispatch.com/article/20150327/NEWS/303279748|archive-date=September 21, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2021, DeWine opposed a bill that would have banned transgender athletes from playing on sports teams that do not match their sex at birth, saying, "This issue is best addressed outside of government, through individual sports leagues and athletic associations, including the [[Ohio High School Athletic Association]], who can tailor policies to meet the needs of their member athletes and member institutions."<ref>{{Cite web|date=June 25, 2021|title=Ohio Governor Mike DeWine opposes state law on transgender athletes|url=https://www.nbc4i.com/news/politics/ohio-statehouse-newsroom/gov-mike-dewine-opposes-state-law-on-transgender-athletes/|access-date=November 1, 2021|website=NBC4 WCMH-TV|language=en-US|archive-date=November 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211101183137/https://www.nbc4i.com/news/politics/ohio-statehouse-newsroom/gov-mike-dewine-opposes-state-law-on-transgender-athletes/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last1=Tobias|first1=Andrew J.|date=June 25, 2021|title=Gov. Mike DeWine criticizes Ohio House bill that would ban transgender athletes from playing girls' sports|url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2021/06/gov-mike-dewine-criticizes-ohio-house-bill-that-would-ban-transgender-athletes-from-playing-girls-sports.html|access-date=November 1, 2021|website=cleveland|language=en|archive-date=November 1, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211101183136/https://www.cleveland.com/open/2021/06/gov-mike-dewine-criticizes-ohio-house-bill-that-would-ban-transgender-athletes-from-playing-girls-sports.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In December 2023, he vetoed a bill that would have banned minors from receiving [[Transgender health care|gender-affirming care]] in Ohio and transgender youth from playing on sports teams that did not match their sex assigned at birth. He said that if the bill became law, "Ohio would be saying that the state—that the government knows better what is medically best for a child than the two people who love that child the most, their parents."<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 29, 2023 |title=Gov. DeWine vetoes Ohio's transgender athlete, healthcare bill |url=https://www.nbc4i.com/news/politics/will-gov-dewine-sign-ohios-transgender-athlete-healthcare-bill-into-law/ |access-date=December 29, 2023 |website=NBC4 WCMH-TV |language=en-US |archive-date=December 29, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231229162235/https://www.nbc4i.com/news/politics/will-gov-dewine-sign-ohios-transgender-athlete-healthcare-bill-into-law/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://apnews.com/article/transgender-health-ohio-minors-veto-c615cafed4fc81d32010d47d8853efaf | title=Ohio's GOP governor vetoes ban on gender-affirming care and transgender athletes in girls' sports | website=[[Associated Press News]] | date=December 29, 2023 }}</ref> In January 2024, the Republican-dominated legislature overrode DeWine's veto of the gender-affirming care ban.<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 10, 2024 |title=Ohio House overrides Republican governor's veto of ban on gender affirming care for minors |url=https://apnews.com/article/transgender-minors-affirming-care-veto-628fdfafecf59c7a0d489756280e5abd |access-date=February 24, 2024 |website=AP News |language=en |archive-date=February 24, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240224212504/https://apnews.com/article/transgender-minors-affirming-care-veto-628fdfafecf59c7a0d489756280e5abd |url-status=live }}</ref> Also in January 2024, DeWine signed an [[executive order]] that restricted gender-affirming surgery for youth<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 8, 2024 |title=After Vetoing Gender-Affirming Care Ban, Ohio Gov. Signs Even Harsher Executive Order |url=https://www.them.us/story/ohio-trans-health-care-executive-order-mike-dewine |access-date=January 19, 2024 |website=them. |language=en-US |archive-date=January 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240119060807/https://www.them.us/story/ohio-trans-health-care-executive-order-mike-dewine |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=January 10, 2024|title=Mike DeWine Could Have Been a GOP Hero for Trans Rights
|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/opinion/articles/2024-01-10/ohio-lawmakers-override-dewine-s-veto-of-bill-banning-gender-affirming-care?embedded-checkout=true |access-date=January 19, 2024 |website=Bloomberg |language=en-US}}</ref> while also proposing new administrative rules focused on transgender youth and adults.<ref>{{Cite web|date=January 11, 2024|title=Ohio moves to restrict transgender healthcare, sports participation|url=https://athensindependent.com/ohio-transgender-healthcare/|access-date=January 19, 2024|website=Athens County Independent|language=en-US|archive-date=January 19, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240119060807/https://athensindependent.com/ohio-transgender-healthcare/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=January 11, 2024|title=Ohio transgender adults speak against proposed administrative rules that would change health care|url=https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2024/01/18/ohio-transgender-adults-speak-against-proposed-administrative-rules-that-would-change-health-care/#:~:text=DeWine%20announced%20two%20proposed%20rules,counseling%20before%20receiving%20more%20treatment.|access-date=January 19, 2024|website=Ohio Capital Journal|language=en-US|archive-date=January 19, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240119060807/https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2024/01/18/ohio-transgender-adults-speak-against-proposed-administrative-rules-that-would-change-health-care/#:~:text=DeWine%20announced%20two%20proposed%20rules,counseling%20before%20receiving%20more%20treatment.|url-status=live}}</ref> In February 2024, after backlash from trans people and healthcare providers, DeWine's administration dropped all the proposed rules to restrict treatment for transgender adults.<ref>{{cite web |first1=Geoff|last1=Mulvihill|first2=Hannah|last2=Schoenbaum|url=https://apnews.com/article/ohio-transgender-genderaffirming-regulations-adults-dewine-179092da5e1bf7c23f7141832e39d87d|title=Ohio backs off proposed restrictions on gender-affirming care for adults|website=[[Associated Press|AP News]]|date=February 7, 2024|access-date=March 10, 2024}}</ref> In November 2024, DeWine signed into law a bill prohibiting students in public schools from using restrooms other than those for the gender they were assigned at birth.<ref name="Tobias">{{cite web |last1=Tobias |first1=Andrew |title=Ohio Gov. Mike DeWine signs transgender bathroom bill |url=https://signalcleveland.org/ohio-gov-mike-dewine-signs-transgender-bathroom-bill/ |website=Signal Cleveland |access-date=November 28, 2024 |date=November 27, 2024 |archive-date=November 27, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241127150859/https://signalcleveland.org/ohio-gov-mike-dewine-signs-transgender-bathroom-bill/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Marijuana===
In 2019, DeWine said: "it would really be a mistake for Ohio, by legislation, to say that marijuana for adults is just OK." In February 2020, [[NORML]], a group advocating the [[marijuana legalization|legalization of marijuana]], gave DeWine an "F" rating in relation to his policies.<ref name="D">[https://www.cleveland.com/open/2020/02/gov-mike-dewine-gets-f-in-marijuana-policy-report-card.html "Gov. Mike DeWine gets F in marijuana policy report card"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200313220940/https://www.cleveland.com/open/2020/02/gov-mike-dewine-gets-f-in-marijuana-policy-report-card.html|date=March 13, 2020}}, ''[[Cleveland.com]]'', Laura Hancock (February 6, 2020)</ref> He opposed [[2023 Ohio Issue 2]], the "Regulate Marijuana Like Alcohol initiative".<ref name=Joyce>{{Cite web|last=Rowland|first=Darren|title=DeWine condemns pot issue on fall ballot: 'This is not your grandfather's marijuana'|url=https://abc6onyourside.com/news/local/dewine-condemns-pot-issue-this-is-not-your-grandfathers-marijuana-columbus-ohio-november-vote-legalization|website=[[NBC]] Channel 6|date=August 17, 2023|access-date=December 30, 2023|archive-date=December 30, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231230154620/https://abc6onyourside.com/news/local/dewine-condemns-pot-issue-this-is-not-your-grandfathers-marijuana-columbus-ohio-november-vote-legalization|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2024, DeWine requested lawmakers take action against [[Δ-8-Tetrahydrocannabinol|Delta 8]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://local12.com/news/local/decisions-on-delta-8-ban-in-indiana-expected-any-day-following-lawsuit | title=Decisions on Delta 8 ban in Indiana expected following lawsuit | date=January 19, 2024 }}</ref>

=== Hamas–Israel war campus protests ===
{{Main|2024 Ohio State University pro-Palestinian campus protests}}

After the April 25, 2024, [[2024 Ohio State University pro-Palestinian campus protests|Ohio State University Gaza Solidarity Encampment]] resulted in at least 36 arrests of pro-Palestinian demonstrators, including 16 [[Ohio State University]] students,<ref name=EncampmentArrests>{{Cite news |last=Farkas |first=Karen |date=April 25, 2024 |title=Hamas-Israel War: Gaza Protest at Ohio State University Campus |url=https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/local/2024/04/25/hamas-israel-war-gaza-protest-ohio-state-university-campus/73456832007/ |work=The Columbus Dispatch |access-date=June 9, 2024 |archive-date=July 2, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240702032828/https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/local/2024/04/25/hamas-israel-war-gaza-protest-ohio-state-university-campus/73456832007/ |url-status=live }}</ref> DeWine said, "I think that Ohio State did well", "what we don't want is any kind of hate", and that he opposes protests "right outside the door of a classroom".<ref name=DeWine>{{cite web |title=More arrested at OSU following protests |url=https://spectrumnews1.com/oh/columbus/news/2024/04/26/more-arrested-at-osu-following-protests |website=Spectrum News 1 |access-date=June 13, 2024 |date=April 26, 2024 |archive-date=June 13, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240613083131/https://spectrumnews1.com/oh/columbus/news/2024/04/26/more-arrested-at-osu-following-protests |url-status=live }}</ref> The demonstrators at the encampment were on the South Oval, which is not in the vicinity of any classrooms.<ref>{{cite web |title=Inside the five-hour standoff between police and protesters on the South Oval |url=https://www.thelantern.com/2024/04/inside-the-five-hour-standoff-between-police-and-protesters-on-the-south-oval/ |website=The Lantern |date=April 2024 |access-date=June 10, 2024 |archive-date=June 11, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240611034234/https://www.thelantern.com/2024/04/inside-the-five-hour-standoff-between-police-and-protesters-on-the-south-oval/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=EncampmentArrests /><ref>{{cite Instagram|user=pslcolumbus|postid=C6MxzAXP-lk|title=PSL Encampment Instagram Post |url=https://www.instagram.com/p/C6MxzAXP-lk/ |access-date=June 9, 2024 |date=April 25, 2024}}</ref> DeWine also said he supported the arrests at Ohio State in the form of [[Ohio State Highway Patrol]], which included an officer who aimed a rifle at protesters during the encampment's dispersal.<ref>{{cite web |title=University says officers had readied firearms, directed toward protesters from Ohio Union's roof once arrests began |url=https://www.thelantern.com/2024/04/university-says-officers-had-readied-firearms-directed-toward-protesters-from-ohio-unions-roof-once-arrests-began/ |website=The Lantern |date=April 2024 |access-date=June 9, 2024 |archive-date=April 29, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240429183658/https://www.thelantern.com/2024/04/university-says-officers-had-readied-firearms-directed-toward-protesters-from-ohio-unions-roof-once-arrests-began/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He said that he supports Ohio and the U.S. "backing the country of Israel".<ref name=DeWine /> In the same interview, he expressed support for [[Anti-BDS laws|Ohio Revised Code Section 9.76]], which legally prohibits Ohio State from boycotting or divesting from companies on the basis of their support for Israel.

===Other===
In 2020, DeWine signed a bill that forbids colleges and universities in Ohio blocking controversial speakers.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cleveland.com/open/2020/12/gov-mike-dewine-signs-a-bill-to-prohibit-ohio-colleges-universities-from-blocking-controversial-speakers.html|title=Gov. Mike DeWine signs a bill to prohibit Ohio colleges, universities from blocking controversial speakers|first=cleveland com|last=Laura Hancock|date=December 18, 2020|website=cleveland|access-date=January 2, 2021|archive-date=December 24, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201224084129/https://www.cleveland.com/open/2020/12/gov-mike-dewine-signs-a-bill-to-prohibit-ohio-colleges-universities-from-blocking-controversial-speakers.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The same year, DeWine's compensation was 17th among state governors, at $159,189, compared to a maximum of $225,000 for the governor of New York and a minimum of $70,000 for the governor of Maine. The Ohio Checkbook shows that 92 employees of the Ohio state teachers retirement system, including director William Neville, equal or exceed the governor's salary.

=== Illegal immigration and immigration reform ===
In an August 10, 2026, opinion article published in ''[[The New York Times]]'', DeWine advocated for immigration reform that combines stricter enforcement against illegal immigration with expanded legal immigration. According to DeWine, President Trump is in a unique position to pursue such reform because of his strong stance on border security. DeWine argued that the US needs immigrants to address an aging population, labor shortages, and the demand for skilled workers in strategic sectors. He proposed measures such as making it easier for qualified foreign students to remain in the country, reforming visas for skilled workers, and maintaining protections for immigrants facing [[Humanitarian crisis|humanitarian crises]]. DeWine wrote that securing the borders while expanding legal immigration could strengthen the US economy, its workforce, and its long-term competitiveness.<ref>{{Cite news |last= DeWine |first= Mike |date= 2026-08-10 |title= Opinion {{!}} Mike DeWine: Trump Would Shock Everyone With This Reform |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/10/opinion/trump-dewine-immigration-reform.html |access-date= 2026-08-15 |work= The New York Times |language= en-US |issn= 0362-4331}}</ref>

==Personal life==
DeWine lives in the [[Whitelaw Reid House]].<ref>{{cite news|date=September 17, 2016|title=Ohio Attorney General Opens His Historic Home to the Public|publisher=WOUB|url=https://woub.org/2016/09/17/ohio-attorney-general-opens-his-historic-home-to-the-public/|access-date=October 28, 2020|archive-date=November 2, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201102114412/https://woub.org/2016/09/17/ohio-attorney-general-opens-his-historic-home-to-the-public/|url-status=live}}</ref> He married Frances Struewing on June 3, 1967, and they have had eight children together. Their daughter Rebecca died at the age of 22 on August 4, 1993, in a car accident.<ref name="The BG News September 7, 1993">{{cite news|date=September 7, 1993|title=Patrol say DeWine's daughter driving too fast|work=The BG News|format=PDF|url=http://scholarworks.bgsu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=6561&context=bg-news|url-status=live|access-date=October 2, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006073426/http://scholarworks.bgsu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=6561&context=bg-news|archive-date=October 6, 2014|quote=Lt. Gov. Michael DeWine's daughter was driving too fast for the wet road conditions when she was killed in a collision, the State Highway Patrol said Monday. Trooper D.T. Heard at the Xenia post said the patrol determined that Rebecca A. DeWine was driving 55 mph on Aug. 4 when her car went across the center line on a curve. The car hit a pickup truck going 39 mph on U.S. 42 north of Xenia, Heard said Monday. The speed recommended on the curve is 25 mph, he said.|via=ScholarWorks@BGSU (Vol 76, Issue 10)}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Laviola|first=Erin|date=October 11, 2018|title=Frances Struewing DeWine, Mike DeWine's Wife: 5 Fast&nbsp;Facts|url=https://heavy.com/news/2018/10/frances-struewing-mike-dewine-wife/|url-status=live|access-date=October 12, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181012033442/https://heavy.com/news/2018/10/frances-struewing-mike-dewine-wife/|archive-date=October 12, 2018}}</ref> One of their sons, [[Pat DeWine]], is an [[Ohio Supreme Court]] Justice. Another son, Brian, is the president of the [[Minor League Baseball]] team the [[Asheville Tourists]]; the DeWine family purchased the team in 2010.<ref>{{cite news|date=January 5, 2010|title=DeWine group to purchase Tourists|work=[[Minor League Baseball]]|url=https://www.milb.com/milb/news/gcs-7873874|url-status=live|access-date=June 7, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612141845/https://www.milb.com/milb/news/dewine-group-to-purchase-tourists/c-7873874|archive-date=June 12, 2018}}</ref> DeWine's second cousin, [[Kevin DeWine]], is the former Ohio Republican Party chairman.

